								<span class="post-item__date">Posted by <a href="https://blogs.transparent.com/japanese/author/ginny/" title="Posts by Ginny" rel="author">Ginny</a> on Jan 19, 2010 in <a href="https://blogs.transparent.com/japanese/category/grammar/" rel="category tag">Grammar</a></span>
							</h2>
			<div class="post-item__entry">
				<p><strong>ã„ã„</strong> (good)&nbsp;which is an<strong> ã„</strong> adjective, is irregular, but only in certain cases.</p>
<p>For example, in the present affirmative polite, <strong>ã„ã„</strong> will be <strong>ã„ã„ã§ã™</strong></p>
<p>However in the present negative polite, <strong>ã„ã„</strong> will be <strong>ã‚ˆãã‚ã‚Šã¾ã›ã‚“</strong></p>
<p>Similarly, in the past affirmative polite, <strong>&nbsp;ã„ã„</strong> will be <strong>ã‚ˆã‹ã£ãŸã§ã™</strong></p>
<p>And the past negative polite, it will be <strong>ã‚ˆãã‚ã‚Šã¾ã›ã‚“ã§ã—ãŸ</strong></p>
<p>When using<strong> ã„</strong> adjectives, you can place the adjective before the noun:</p>
<p><strong>ã“ã‚Œã¯ã€€ã„ã„ã€€ã‚³ãƒ¼ãƒ’ãƒ¼ã§ã™</strong> = This is good coffee.</p>
<p>(<strong>ã“ã‚Œ</strong> = this. <strong>ã¯</strong> =&nbsp;subject marking&nbsp;particle.ã€€<strong>ã„ã„</strong> = goodã€€<strong>ã‚³ãƒ¼ãƒ’ãƒ¼ </strong>= coffee. <strong>ã§ã™</strong> = is.)</p>
<p>You can also place an <strong>ã„</strong> adjective after the noun it modifies:</p>
<p><strong>ãã®ã€€ã¿ãšã¯ã€€ã¤ã‚ãŸã„ã§ã™</strong> = That water is cold.</p>
<p>(<strong>ãã®</strong> = that.ã€€<strong>ã¿ãš</strong> = water. <strong>ã¯</strong> = subject marking particle.ã€€<strong>ã¤ã‚ãŸã„</strong> = cold. <strong>ã§ã™</strong> = is.)</p>
<p>Like with<strong> ã„</strong> adjectives, you place <strong>ãª</strong> adjectives before nouns:</p>
<p><strong>ã’ã‚“ããªã€€ã²ã¨ã§ã™</strong> =&nbsp;[He/Sheâ€™s] a lively/energetic person</p>
<p>(<strong>ã’ã‚“ããª </strong>= lively/energetic.ã€€<strong>ã²ã¨</strong> = person. <strong>ã§ã™</strong> = is.)</p>
<p>However, when <strong>ãª</strong> adjectives come after a noun, leave off the <strong>ãª</strong>:</p>
<p><strong>ã‹ã‚Œã¯ã€€ã³ã‚“ã¼ã†ã§ã™</strong> = Heâ€™s poor</p>
<p>(<strong>ã‹ã‚Œ</strong> = he.<strong> ã¯</strong> = subject marking particle.ã€€<strong>ã³ã‚“ã¼ã†</strong> = poor. <strong>ã§ã™</strong> = is.&nbsp;)</p>
<p><strong>ãª</strong> adjectives are considered quasi adjectival nouns and thatâ€™s why the <strong>ãª</strong> is left off after the noun.</p>
<p>When you have a situation like this, how do you know whether the adjective is an<strong> ã„</strong> or <strong>ãª</strong> adjective?</p>
<p><strong>ã¯ãªã¯ã€€ãã‚Œã„ã§ã™</strong> = The flower is pretty</p>
<p>(<strong>ã¯ãª</strong> = flower. <strong>ã¯</strong> = subject marking particle.ã€€<strong>ãã‚Œã„</strong> = pretty. <strong>ã§ã™</strong> = is.)</p>
<p>The answer is that you just have to memorize that <strong>ã„ã‚Œã„</strong>(<strong>ãª</strong>) is a <strong>ãª</strong> adjective. Luckily there arenâ€™t that many adjectives that are ambiguous as <strong>ã„ã‚Œã„</strong>(<strong>ãª</strong>).</p>
